import Docs from '@/layouts/docs.astro';
import Snippet from '@/components/product/Snippet.astro';
---
<Docs title="Audit & observability">
  <div class="doc-title">
    <span class="section-kicker">EVIDENCE / OPERATIONS</span>
    <h1>Keep the decision inspectable.</h1>
    <p>Audit, metrics, and telemetry serve different purposes. Configure and validate each surface you rely on.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Record format</h2>
  <p>DecisionRecord contains id, timestamp, effect, policies, principal, action, resource, reasons, request/session identifiers, and optional agent-chain, trace, tenant, subject, and authenticated-actor fields. Authenticated standalone PDP requests record the bound caller identity, API-key id, tenant, and whether the privileged <code>authorize:any</code> scope was used. This provenance is separate from the evaluated principal and Cedar context. JSONL, CEF, LEEF, and ECS exports retain it. A chained record adds chain_id, prev_hash, and record_hash. These records describe authorization decisions, not proof that the tool executed successfully.</p>
  <Snippet title="Illustrative audit fields · abbreviated, not a complete record" lang="json" code={JSON.stringify({principal:'research',action:'repo_read',resource:'demo',effect:'allow',authenticated_actor:{subject_type:'Agent',subject_id:'console-service',credential_id:'key-123',can_act_as:true},policies:['policy0'],timestamp:'2026-09-21T12:00:00Z',prev_hash:'illustrative previous hash',record_hash:'illustrative HMAC'},null,2)}/>

  <h2>Enable and verify the chain</h2>
  <p>Set AGENTGUARD_CHAIN_SECRET to a protected secret file before starting the server or CLI authorization. Without a secret, the log is plain JSONL. HMAC-SHA256 chains records using canonical serialization. Keep the original log and .chainid sidecar together.</p>
  <Snippet title="Audit operations" code={'agentguard audit verify --audit .audit/decisions.jsonl --secret-file .chain-secret\nagentguard audit export --format jsonl --out decisions.jsonl\nagentguard audit export --format cef --out decisions.cef\nagentguard audit export --format leef --out decisions.leef\nagentguard audit export --format ecs --out decisions.ecs.jsonl'}/>
  <p>Exports reformat records for downstream ingestion; there are no managed Splunk, Elastic, or other vendor connectors implied. Configure your own collector, retention, and access controls. Exporting does not substitute for verifying the original chain.</p>

  <h2>Integrity limits</h2>
  <p>The HMAC secret is trusted. An attacker with that key can forge records. A valid chain alone does not prove completeness: keep independent trusted head checkpoints to detect truncation or wholesale replacement. Rotation and pseudonymization require preserving original evidence; audit erase changes records and invalidates their original authentication.</p>
  <p>Server handlers return an error when a configured audit append fails. Embedders can omit audit configuration, and the core authorizer does not log automatically. The CLI can skip audit and prints its decision before appending; callers must wait for a successful process exit.</p>

  <h2 id="observability">Trace context and metrics</h2>
  <p>HTTP middleware reads traceparent, creates a child span identifier, and returns x-agentguard-span-id. The single-evaluation handler copies trace context into the request for audit correlation. Do not assume identical correlation or metrics behavior across batch, gRPC, and embedded paths.</p>
  <Snippet title="Health and Prometheus endpoints" code={'curl http://127.0.0.1:8443/healthz\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8443/readyz\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:8443/metrics'}/>
  <p>The telemetry Metrics type tracks decision count/duration, cache activity, policy reloads, and PDP errors. These are in-memory counters, not a durable audit trail. Scraping requires your monitoring infrastructure. Health/metrics routes are not protected by decision-route authentication.</p>

  <h2>OpenTelemetry and OTLP</h2>
  <p>agentguard-telemetry exposes Sink and SinkEvent with stdout, JSONL, and feature-gated OtlpSink implementations. The OTLP sink exports OpenTelemetry log records over gRPC, not an automatically installed distributed-tracing pipeline.</p>
  <p>Construct the sink in your embedding application, emit events, and flush/shut it down. OtlpSink::from_env reads OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and OTEL_SERVICE_NAME. Standalone server startup configures tracing-subscriber logging but does not instantiate this sink.</p>
  <p>Actual exported attributes include <code>agentguard.effect</code>, <code>agentguard.principal</code>, <code>agentguard.action</code>, <code>agentguard.resource</code>, <code>agentguard.cached</code>, and optional <code>trace.id</code>, <code>span.id</code>, and <code>agentguard.tenant_id</code>. Do not assume an authz.* semantic convention that is not emitted by this code.</p>
  <p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-core/src/decision/record.rs">record schema</a>, <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-core/src/decision/chain.rs">chain implementation</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-telemetry/src/sinks/otlp.rs">OTLP exporter</a>.</p>
</Docs>
